summary:
Critic, lens: whether the mechanical checks hold up. I reviewed /tmp/claude-1000/-home-zhengyew-telelean/20626c17-0334-4498-ad86-9dc24b7010ed/scratchpad/plans/slice4-plan.md against the spec v4.1, the user's turn-20/21 words, the coverage audit, slice-3's plan and slice-2's plan. Slice 3 is not built, so most of this compares plan with plan. Slice 2's built rules were read where they settle a baseline (core/src/gate/sessionRules.ts, gate/g0.ts, markup/decls.ts), and Lean 4.35's sources for coercion expansion (Lean/Meta/Coe.lean:44-66), `seal`/`unseal` (Init/Notation.lean:1045,1054) and `register_simp_attr`.

The design puts S0 before telelean's typed write, owns typed content unit by unit, and protects it with TransformFreezes. That leaves two blocker gaps.
- **Meaning:** what the person's typed Lean means is never compared with what they typed. While its queue runs, a TransformFreeze compares text only and holds nothing; it then takes its value from the end of the queue, a state Claude shaped. D4-23 claims RV-5 covers instances; that is wrong for coercions, because Lean's expandCoe removes the instance from the elaborated term. Claude can make a typed axiom assert something else, and it is accepted as the person's with no Accept.
- **Removals:** nothing makes the typed write's own effects stay in place outside the field keys of cards that still exist. Every rule reads S0 or B. So Claude can put back a trashed card's statement and `.holds` as declarations with no card. That is S9's hidden belief: the gate, ownership and the backstop all pass it, and it also gets around TR-13's dependents.

Major findings:
- the ownership of a new card's block contradicts itself (one reading counts Claude's fills as the person's);
- no RV-5 edges for typed content on Claude's Add cards;
- the Submit cascade misses accepted items whose covered fields the person types, and §3.9 contradicts §3.11 on this;
- the open-Lean-statement flows the plan itself recommends can never pass (mark_proof_broken is narrowed to listed dependents; the docstring-mirror update freezes the open statement);
- transform freezes are given to follow-on edits and cleared proofs;
- TR-12 (b) refuses valid edits and passes Lean that breaks where it is written (session names in declarations; the scratch's context).

Minor findings cover the order and command detection of TR-12 (a), Submit's take-out, how freezes interact with Reject, the rework scope, a race on the direct path, refusals that come only at Submit review, tool definitions, lemma cards and the definition of no-proofs.

findings.problem:
The scratch is a different context from where the typed write lands:
- it imports the whole library, so a changed declaration is checked against declarations it cannot see in place (later in its module, or in modules downstream);
- it declares a changed type a second time in a new namespace beside B's old one, instead of replacing it;
- it orders new vocabulary by draft order rather than by what uses what.
On the direct path TR-12 (b) is the only Lean check, so anything it passes is written to the person's files.
